      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{4}5{e}^{-x/4}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "If we let $u=-\\frac{x}{4},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,-4\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-1$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}5{e}^{-x/4}\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{-x/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{-1}{e}^{u}\\cdot (-4)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-20{\\int }_{0}^{-1}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-20\\cdot {e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{-1} \\\\  & =-20({e}^{-1}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =-20({e}^{-1}-1) \\\\  & =20-20{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
