    "q-30975": {
      "dom_id": "step-q30975",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "If we let $u=-2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration,we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-2$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{-2}{2}^{u}\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{-2}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{2}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}){|}_{0}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\frac{1}{4}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
