    "q-349627": {
      "dom_id": "step-q349627",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}2\\cdot {3}^{6-3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "If we let $u=6-3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}2\\cdot {3}^{6-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{0}2\\cdot {3}^{u}(-\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{3}^{0}{3}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{{3}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}){|}_{3}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{0}-{3}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(1-27) \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
